  abstract = {An exact asymptotic farfield solution of the plane-wave Burgers' equation is derived for the case of a dual-frequency parametric source. The influence of spherical spreading losses is then included by matching the solution at low primary wave amplitudes with solutions obtained by the method of successive approximation. From the matched solution, the referred pressure of the difference-frequency component at 1 m from the source (i. e. , the equivalent difference-frequency source level) is derived as a universal function of scaled primary-wave amplitudes, frequencies, source dimensions, and physical constants of the medium. This function, which defines the saturation limit of a parametric array more completely than previous approximate models, permits the maximum conversion efficiency of the source to be evaluated for a particular set of input parameters. The amplitude dependence of the directivity function and directivity index for spreading loss controlled arrays can also be determined. Comparison of the characteristic with data published in the literature shows good agreement over quite a wide range of scaled primary-wave source parameters.},
  file = {C\:\\Users\\MrJia\\Zotero\\storage\\TV492EMU\\Fenlon_2005_Nonlinear scaling for saturation‐limited parametric arrays.pdf;C\:\\Users\\MrJia\\Zotero\\storage\\SDJEZXNP\\Nonlinear-scaling-for-saturation-limited.html}
