  abstract = {In this report we discuss the solution of the following matrix problem: A {$\cdot$} x = {$\lambda$} x, where A is a nxn matrix, which may have complex eigenvalues. Matrix problems of this form occur frequently in the solution of differential equations in the physical sciences and engineering. The scalar {$\lambda$} is called an eigenvalue of A if there exists a nonzero column vector x that is a solution to the following system of linear equations: A {$\cdot$} x = {$\lambda$} x. The solution vector x is called the right eigenvector, and the scalar {$\lambda$} is called its associated eigenvalue. In this report we explore various analytically methods for determining a bi-orthogonality relationship between the left and right eigenvectors of A. We will see that when the eigenvectors are complex (associated with complex eigenvalues) care must be taken in working with the above definitions. The contents of this report are the essential building blocks for studying more advanced topics in linear algebra, numerical methods, differential equations, and problems involving the theory of bifurcations and stability of dynamical systems.},
