  abstract = {Open physical systems with balanced loss and gain, described by non-Hermitian parity-time \$\${\textbackslash}left( \{\{{\textbackslash}cal P\}\{{\textbackslash}cal T\}\} {\textbackslash}right)\$\$reflection symmetric Hamiltonians, exhibit a transition which could engender modes that exponentially decay or grow with time, and thus spontaneously breaks the \$\$\{{\textbackslash}cal P\}\{{\textbackslash}cal T\}\$\$-symmetry. Such \$\$\{{\textbackslash}cal P\}\{{\textbackslash}cal T\}\$\$-symmetry-breaking transitions have attracted many interests because of their extraordinary behaviors and functionalities absent in closed systems. Here we report on the observation of \$\$\{{\textbackslash}cal P\}\{{\textbackslash}cal T\}\$\$-symmetry-breaking transitions by engineering time-periodic dissipation and coupling, which are realized through state-dependent atom loss in an optical dipole trap of ultracold 6Li atoms. Comparing with a single transition appearing for static dissipation, the time-periodic counterpart undergoes \$\$\{{\textbackslash}cal P\}\{{\textbackslash}cal T\}\$\$-symmetry breaking and restoring transitions at vanishingly small dissipation strength in both single and multiphoton transition domains, revealing rich phase structures associated to a Floquet open system. The results enable ultracold atoms to be a versatile tool for studying \$\$\{{\textbackslash}cal P\}\{{\textbackslash}cal T\}\$\$-symmetric quantum systems.},
