  abstract = {The noise which is caused by vibration of steel plate girders of a motorway is studied. This type of noise is often referred to as elevated structure noise, and has its main component within the frequency range 500 Hz{\textpm}1 kHz. Since a steel plate girder can be considered as a rectangular plate with an extreme aspect ratio, the sound {\textregistered}eld radiated by a baed elastic plate strip of in{\textregistered}nite length, which is the simplest model of a steel plate girder, was theoretically analysed in the preceding paper (Sakagami, K., Michishita, K., Morimoto, M., and Kitamura, Y. Sound radiation from a baed elastic plate strip of in{\textregistered}nite length with various concentrated exitation forces. Appl Acoust 55: 181{\textpm}202). However, a real motorway construction is more appropriately modelled by an unbaed strip. Thus the unbaed condition has been analysed numerically in this paper. The method of equivalent sources is used. The main features of the radiated sound {\textregistered}eld are discussed in the light of numerical examples, and compared with those of a baed strip. Two vibration patterns, i.e. an idealised piston vibration and {\textasciimacron}exural vibration excited by a moment are considered. Furthermore, in order to determine the far-{\textregistered}eld range of an in{\textregistered}nitely long plate, the variation of the radiated sound {\textregistered}eld with the distance is discussed. \# 2000 Elsevier Science Ltd. All rights reserved.},
