  abstract = {An echolocation method is proposed, utilizing the non-directional characteristics of demodulated low-frequency echoes generated by the ultrasound from parametric acoustic array. Compared to existing researches, the proposed method can estimate the direct distance to reflection point using one microphone, even when the incident wave is not perpendicular to the reflective surface. Moreover, the microphone does not need to be positioned along the strict specular reflection path of incident wave. To combat false estimations, an algorithm is developed, combining time-domain and frequency-domain estimates to match the chirp signal's slope. The proposed method utilizes both the sharp directionality of incident ultrasound and the wide-beam angle of low-frequency echo. In distance measurement experiments, the method maintained an average accuracy of 88\% even when the angle between the incident wave and the reflection point's normal vector reached 36{$^\circ$}. In mapping experiments, the method exhibited higher efficiency than existing studies, while maintaining comparable accuracy.},
